Spec: SpecDD Parser Foundation

Platform: IntelliJ Platform / Kotlin

Purpose:
  Provide shared line-level SpecDD classification for editor features.

Structure:
  SpecDDLanguageFacts.kt: Known SpecDD language facts shared by editor concerns.
  SpecDDLineClassifier.kt: Line classifier, classification model, and task status model.

Owns:
  SpecDDLanguageFacts.kt
  SpecDDLineClassifier.kt

Can modify:
  SpecDDLanguageFacts.kt
  SpecDDLineClassifier.kt

Can read:
  ../../../../../../test/kotlin/ai/specdd/idea/parser/*

Must:
  Define known SpecDD section labels.
  Define known scenario step keywords.
  Define supported task marker bodies.
  Classify lines independently from IntelliJ editor colors, inspections, and annotations.
  Classify one caller-provided line range at a time.
  Preserve original line offsets in every classification.
  Report the first non-whitespace offset as `contentStart`.
  Classify whitespace-only line ranges as blank lines.
  Classify lines whose first non-whitespace character is `#` as comments.
  Classify comments before task markers, section headers, scenario steps, key-value lines, and text.
  Classify known section headers before task markers, scenario steps, key-value lines, and text.
  Classify a section header only when a known section label is followed immediately by `:`.
  Keep section header classification independent from whether inline content after `:` is valid.
  Report section label, colon, value start, and value end offsets.
  Preserve inline section value whitespace exactly in the reported value range.
  Keep section label ordering longest/specific enough that multi-word labels such as `Must not` and `Can modify` are recognized before shorter overlapping labels.
  Classify task marker lines before scenario steps, key-value lines, and text.
  Recognize supported three-character task markers `[x]`, `[X]`, `[ ]`, `[!]`, `[?]`, and `[-]`.
  Map `[x]` and `[X]` to done task status.
  Map `[ ]` to open task status.
  Map `[!]` to blocked task status.
  Map `[?]` to question task status.
  Map `[-]` to skipped task status.
  Recognize bracketed unsupported task markers such as `[invalid]` as invalid task status.
  Recognize unsupported single-character task marker bodies such as `[z]` as invalid task status.
  Report task marker start and end offsets.
  Recognize an optional task id only when non-whitespace text after the task marker starts with `#` followed by at least one digit.
  Report task id start and end offsets when a numeric task id is present.
  Ignore empty hash ids such as `#` with no digits.
  Classify scenario step lines before key-value lines and text.
  Recognize scenario step keywords `Given`, `When`, `Then`, `And`, and `But`.
  Require a scenario step keyword to be followed by end of line or whitespace.
  Leave words that merely start with a scenario step keyword as text.
  Classify generic body key-value lines before text.
  Recognize generic key-value lines only when the first `:` has a nonempty key before it and at least one space after it.
  Allow generic key-value keys and values to contain any valid UTF-8 text.
  Do not require key case or a key character class.
  Require generic key-value colons to be preceded by a non-whitespace key character.
  Report generic key, colon, value start, and value end offsets.
  Classify all otherwise unrecognized nonblank lines as text.
  Expose classification payloads only for the matching line kind.
  Keep the parser facts usable by highlighting and validation without depending on either concern.
  Stay independent from syntax highlighting and validation diagnostics.
  Cover blank lines, comments, section headers with and without values, all task states, task ids, scenario steps, key-value lines, and text lines in tests.
  Cover negative cases for step-prefix words, empty hash ids, invalid task marker bodies, and unrecognized text in tests.

Must not:
  Define editor colors.
  Report validation diagnostics.
  Decide whether indentation is valid.
  Decide whether a section is allowed to repeat.
  Decide whether a section supports inline values or body lines.
  Decide whether a known section is in the correct order.
  Emit IntelliJ PSI, annotations, quick fixes, or error severities.
